## Toolchain

The Astral toolchain — keep it unless there is a clear reason to change:

- `uv`   — environment, locking, running
- `ruff` — linting + formatting
- `ty`   — type checking
{%- if use_pytest %}
- `pytest` — testing
- `pytest-cov` — coverage measurement and threshold gate
{%- endif %}

## Commands

```bash
uv sync --group dev      # create/refresh the environment
uv run ruff check .      # lint
uv run ruff format .     # format
uv run ty check          # type-check
{%- if use_pytest %}
uv run pytest            # run tests (coverage included by default)
uv run pytest --no-cov   # skip coverage for quick iteration
{%- endif %}
```
{% if use_pytest %}
Coverage is measured by `pytest-cov` against `{{ package_name }}/` with
branch tracking; the `fail_under` gate lives in `pyproject.toml`
(`0` = measure only — raise it once you have a baseline).
{% endif %}

## Conventions

- Keep code fully typed — `ty` runs with `error-on-warning`.
- Fix `ruff` findings rather than suppressing them, unless there is a
  clear, commented reason.
{%- if use_pytest %}
- Tests live in `tests/` and may use bare `assert` (ruff `S101` is
  waived there).
- Mirror the package layout under `tests/`: `{{ package_name }}/sub/mod.py`
  is tested by `tests/sub/test_mod.py`. Keep `__init__.py` markers in
  test subpackages (matches the `INP` ruff rule).
- Fixtures live in `tests/conftest.py` when you need them — modern
  default, applies to `tests/` only. Use a root `conftest.py` only for
  `pytest_plugins` declarations, doctest fixtures shared with source
  files, or project-wide collection hooks.
- Test layout: flat module-level `def test_*` functions by default;
  reach for a plain `class TestX:` (grouping only, no inheritance) to
  organize a large or multi-feature surface. Don't mix the two styles
  within one file.
- Not every source file needs a dedicated test file — types-only
  modules, re-export `__init__.py` markers, and details covered through
  a public-facing module are intentional exceptions.
- Cross-module test helpers live in `tests/<pkg>/helpers.py`; shared
  fixtures in `tests/fixtures/`; per-package configuration in
  `conftest.py`.
- Test quality: assertions check concrete return values *and* side
  effects, not merely "no exception raised"; error paths use
  `pytest.raises(..., match=...)`; verify numbers against independently
  computed values, not the implementation's own output; make timing/IO
  deterministic (an injected clock, fault injection) rather than flaky.
  A good test fails on *subtle* breakage, not just obvious breakage.
  When a contract is "one batched call per source",
  verify it with a spy, not only by the result.
{%- endif %}
- `ty` has no plugin system; rely on standard typing (PEP 681
  `dataclass_transform`, `.pyi` stubs), not type-checker plugins.
- Suppress `ty` errors with `# ty: ignore[<error-name>]` using `ty`'s
  own error names (e.g. `invalid-argument-type`), not mypy/pyright
  codes. Always include the specific code rather than bare
  `# ty: ignore` — bare suppressions can mask future regressions.
- Error messages name the valid set or the fix, not just the failure
  ("unsupported node type 'X': expected File, Directory, ..."; "balance
  pause/resume, or use `suspend()`"). The message should tell the caller
  what to do next.

## Python style

`ruff` enforces most of this — run `uv run ruff check --fix` rather than
applying it by hand.

- Every module starts with `from __future__ import annotations` (ruff
  isort `required-imports`). Empty `__init__.py` package markers are
  exempt.
- Use builtin generics — `list`, `dict`, `tuple`, `type` — never
  `typing.List`, `typing.Dict`, `typing.Tuple`, `typing.Type` (ruff
  `UP`).
- Imports are grouped and sorted: standard library, third party, first
  party, then a trailing `if TYPE_CHECKING:` block (grouped the same
  way). Within a group `import X` precedes `from X import Y`; entries
  are alphabetical.
- Docstrings are optional — write them where they clarify intent, not
  mechanically. "Mechanically" targets two habits to avoid: comments (or
  docstrings) that merely restate the code, and a base class whose
  docstring explains itself in terms of its specific subclasses — except
  a *closed* hierarchy's base, which may name its subclasses as a
  deliberate family map. It is *not* a licence to leave a consumed
  method bare. When written, document *intent and contracts, not
  mechanism*:
  - Lead with a one-line summary — a declarative noun phrase for classes
    ("An ordered, read-only view over ..."), an imperative verb phrase
    for functions and methods ("Yield successive windows from
    `items`."). Two kinds take a noun phrase instead: a property getter
    ("The reporting unit ...") and a boolean-returning *method*, which
    leads with "Whether ..." ("Whether `path` exists."). A boolean
    *function* stays imperative ("Test whether a path exists.").
  - A concrete public method a caller consumes must be self-explainable
    from its own docstring and signature — never lean on an inherited
    parent docstring. Abstract base methods document only the generic
    contract and never name a specific subclass.
  - Surface what callers cannot infer from the signature alone:
    invariants, edge cases, what subclasses must override, policy
    trade-offs. Skip restating what the code already shows.
  - Use [Google style](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#38-comments-and-docstrings)
    sections (`Args:`, `Returns:`, `Yields:`, `Raises:`,
    `Type Parameters:`); omit types from `Args:` since the signature
    already carries them. Custom sections (`Example:`, `Truth table:`,
    and ad-hoc labels) are welcome when they clarify a real pitfall or
    pattern.
  - Add an `Args:` / `Returns:` block only for what the summary and
    signature cannot already convey. When they make the behaviour
    obvious (a no-arg getter, a self-evident one-liner), the summary
    *is* the whole docstring. Document an edge case shared across a
    family once on the class.
  - Reference identifiers in backticks (`my_method`, `param`,
    `MyClass.method`). Literal option values get backticks too
    (`"merge"`, `"error"`), as identifiers do.
- Within a function body, separate logical groups with a single blank
  line and put a blank line before the final `return`; leave a tightly
  coupled one- or two-line body unbroken.
- In a long module, group related definitions under a boxed comment
  banner — a centred title between two `#`-bordered rules.
- Standalone runnable scripts carry PEP 723 inline metadata (the
  `# /// script` block). `uv` manages it (`uv add --script`); add or
  edit it by hand only when explicitly asked.

## Dependencies

- Change dependencies with `uv`, not by editing `pyproject.toml` by
  hand: `uv add <pkg>` / `uv remove <pkg>` for what the code imports at
  run time, `uv add --group dev <pkg>` for what only tests, linting, or
  type checking need{% if is_library %}, and `uv add --optional <extra> <pkg>` for
  an optional extra{% endif %}. Commit `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`
  together — CI installs with `uv sync --locked` and fails on a stale
  lock.
- To move to a newer release, raise the minimum with
  `uv add "<pkg>>=X.Y.Z"` (keep the `--group`{% if is_library %} or `--optional`{% endif %} flag
  it was added with), or refresh only the lock with
  `uv lock --upgrade-package <pkg>`.
- Raising `ruff`'s minimum also raises `[tool.ruff] required-version`
  and the "omitted (ruff vX.Y.Z)" note at the end of `pyproject.toml`;
  re-check that the sections it lists still use only defaults.
- A runtime dependency's range is a requirement users see, so it goes
  in `CHANGELOG.md`; `dev` group changes stay out (see
  *Documentation → `CHANGELOG.md`*).

### Supported Python versions

The supported versions are stated in several places. Change them
together, in one commit:

- `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml` — the oldest supported version;
- `[tool.ty.environment] python-version` — the same oldest version, so
  `ty` flags syntax and APIs newer than it;
- one `Programming Language :: Python :: X.Y` classifier per supported
  version;
- `.python-version` — the version development and CI run on, any
  supported one;
- the README's Python badge and the requirement line in
  {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %}.

The oldest version is the template's `python_version` answer: move it
with `copier update -d python_version=X.Y`, which re-renders all of the
above and the *Project* line of this file; then add back by hand any
newer versions the project also supports. CI runs `.python-version`
only, so check the oldest version locally before changing it:
{% if use_pytest %}`uv run --isolated --python X.Y pytest`{% else %}`uv run --isolated --python X.Y python -c "import {{ package_name }}"`{% endif %}.
Dropping a version is a `**Breaking:**` `Changed` entry in
`CHANGELOG.md`; supporting a new one is an `Added` entry.

## Documentation

`README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`,{% if include_todo %} `TODO.md`,{% endif %} and this
file are kept current by whoever changes the code — update them in the
same commit as the change they describe, not as a follow-up. A
docs-only fix (wording, a stale example) is its own `📝` commit.

| When a change ...                                         | Update                                   |
| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| alters user-visible behavior, requirements, or dependencies | `CHANGELOG.md` → `[Unreleased]`        |
{%- if is_library %}
| adds, removes, or renames a public module                 | `README.md` → 🧩 Modules                 |
| adds, removes, or changes a command in `[project.scripts]` | `README.md` → ⌨️ CLI                     |
| changes how the package is installed                      | `README.md` → 📦 Installation            |
{%- else %}
| changes how the project is set up                         | `README.md` → 📦 Setup                   |
| changes how the project is run                            | `README.md` → 🚀 Usage                   |
| adds, moves, or removes a top-level directory             | `README.md` → 🗂️ Layout, if present      |
{%- endif %}
| changes code that a README example uses                   | that README → the example                |
| moves, renames, or deletes a file a README links to       | that README → the link                   |
| adds, removes, renames, or reorders a sub-README section  | that README → `## Contents`, if present  |
{%- if include_todo %}
| defers work, or uncovers a problem it does not fix        | `TODO.md` → add an item                  |
| resolves a `TODO.md` item                                 | `TODO.md` → remove the item              |
{%- endif %}
| settles a design choice later work should not reopen      | `docs/decisions/` → add a record         |
| settles a convention later sessions must follow           | `AGENTS.md` → below the marker           |

Each document has one audience and one tense:

- `README.md` describes the project **as it is now** — what it does and
  how to {% if is_library %}install and use it{% else %}set it up and run it{% endif %}. No history, no "new in vX.Y".
- `CHANGELOG.md` records what **changed for a user** of the
  {% if is_library %}library{% else %}application{% endif %} — external behavior only, never internal refactoring.
{%- if include_todo %}
- `TODO.md` lists work that is **not done yet**.
{%- endif %}
- `docs/decisions/` records **why** the code is shaped as it is — one
  decision per file, written once.
- `AGENTS.md` holds rules for whoever works on the code next.

All of them wrap prose at 72 columns. Tables, code blocks, headings,
and a line holding a long URL or command are exempt.

### `CHANGELOG.md`

The format is [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).

- New entries go under `## [Unreleased]`, grouped into these six
  categories and no others, always in this order:

  | Category     | For                                          |
  | ------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
  | `Added`      | new features                                 |
  | `Changed`    | changes in existing functionality            |
  | `Deprecated` | features that will be removed                |
  | `Removed`    | features removed now                         |
  | `Fixed`      | bug fixes                                    |
  | `Security`   | vulnerabilities                              |

  Omit empty categories and never invent new ones: a performance
  improvement is `Changed`, a fixed performance regression is `Fixed`.
  The first entry replaces the `_No entries yet._` placeholder.
- Record only changes to **external behavior** — what someone who
  {% if is_library %}imports the library{% else %}runs the application{% endif %} can observe without reading its source:
{%- if is_library %}
  - the public API: importable names without a leading `_`, their
    signatures, return values, raised exceptions, and warnings;
  - results, side effects, and file or serialization formats;
{%- else %}
  - commands, command-line options, and configuration keys;
  - accepted inputs, produced outputs (files, formats, reports), exit
    codes, and results;
{%- endif %}
  - performance;
  - requirements: supported Python versions and runtime dependency
    ranges.

  Internal refactoring stays out — restructuring, renaming or moving
  {% if is_library %}private{% else %}internal{% endif %} code, removing dead code, tests, CI, dev-tooling and
  dev-dependency bumps, comments, docstrings, and the other Markdown
  documents. The test: would a user of the {% if is_library %}library{% else %}application{% endif %}
  notice the difference, or have to act on it? If a refactor does
  change behavior, however slightly, record the behavior change, not
  the refactor.
- One bullet per change, phrased to read on from its category heading:
  - `Added` and `Removed` lead with *what* as a noun phrase:
    "`CommitRetry`, a frozen retry policy for commit steps refused by a
    transient Windows lock." / "`utils.types`, replaced by PEP 695 type
    parameters."
  - `Changed`, `Deprecated`, `Fixed`, and `Security` make what the user
    sees the subject and describe the new state in the present tense:
    "`load()` now raises `FormatError` instead of `ValueError` on a
    malformed header." / "`StagedFile.commit` no longer fails on a
    transient Windows `PermissionError`."
  - Never open with the heading's own verb ("Added ...", "Fixed ...")
    or an imperative ("Add ...", "Fix ...") — that is commit-message
    style.
- Keep an entry short by default: one or two sentences saying what
  changed. Causes, internal mechanisms, and measurements belong in the
  commit message, not here.
- `**Breaking:**`, `Deprecated`, and `Removed` entries spell out the
  upgrade instead, since that is where a reader has to act. Say what
  changed, what behaved differently before or still works unchanged,
  and what to do now — the replacement or the migration step. A
  `Deprecated` entry also says when the feature goes, if that is known.
- Start every incompatible change with `**Breaking:**`, spelled exactly
  so. It can appear under `Changed`, `Removed`, `Fixed` (a fix that
  breaks code relying on the old behavior), and `Security`; `Added` and
  `Deprecated` never break anything. Pre-1.0, breaking changes ship in a
  minor bump.
{%- if is_library %}
- Before removing or renaming a public name, deprecate it when you can:
  keep it working for at least one release with a `DeprecationWarning`
  and list it under `Deprecated` with its replacement.
{%- else %}
- Before removing or renaming a command, an option, or a configuration
  key, deprecate it when you can: keep it working for at least one
  release with a warning and list it under `Deprecated` with its
  replacement.
{%- endif %}
  The eventual `Removed` entry is still `**Breaking:**` and says which
  version deprecated it ("deprecated since 0.15"). Something that has
  to go at once skips the `Deprecated` step but never the
  `**Breaking:**` mark.
- Backtick identifiers, option values, file names, and commands. Group
  several small changes to one feature as sub-bullets under a single
  **bold** lead-in. Wrap lines at 72 columns.
- `[Unreleased]` describes the net change since the last release. When
  an unreleased change is reworked, reverted, or fixed, edit or delete
  its entry instead of adding a new one — users never saw the
  intermediate state.
- Released sections are history: change them only to correct a factual
  error. A release pulled after publishing keeps its section, marked
  `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD [YANKED]`.
- A changelog written before these rules keeps its released sections
  as they are. Bring only `[Unreleased]` in line, and add the link
  references for every past version.

Cut a release only when the user asks for one. You may suggest it when
`[Unreleased]` has grown long or holds a `**Breaking:**` change. To
promote `[Unreleased]` for release `X.Y.Z`:

1. Re-read the section: merge entries that describe one change and drop
   any whose subject came and went within the cycle.
2. Rename its heading to `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` (ISO 8601 date) and
   add a fresh, empty `## [Unreleased]` above it.
3. Update the link references at the bottom of the file:

   ```
   [Unreleased]: https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/compare/vX.Y.Z...HEAD
   [X.Y.Z]: https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/compare/vPREVIOUS...vX.Y.Z
   ```

   For the first release, `[X.Y.Z]` points to
   `https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/releases/tag/vX.Y.Z` instead.
{%- if include_todo %}

### `TODO.md`

`TODO.md` holds **open work only**: work deliberately deferred — out of
scope for the current change, blocked on a decision, or a limitation
found along the way. It is not a design document, a progress log, or a
record of what is done: decisions go to `docs/decisions/`, finished
work to git history and `CHANGELOG.md`.

- An item is one bullet by default: what needs doing and why, led by
  the relevant path in backticks when there is one — "`io/reader.py`:
  stream large files instead of reading them whole (memory spikes above
  ~1 GB)."
- Only when one line cannot carry it, give the item a `### <what needs
  doing>` heading and a short body: the problem (a code snippet may show
  it), why it was deferred, and a closing `Revisit when ...` line naming
  what should reopen it — a use case that needs it, a measurement that
  would justify it, or the decision it waits on. A body that grows past
  a few short paragraphs is a design question: settle it in a
  `docs/decisions/` record and keep only the remaining work here.
- Items sit under `## Open`, most important first. Once the list
  outgrows a screen, replace `## Open` with `##` headings by area (a
  package or a topic), most important first within each.
- No owners, dates, status markers, checkboxes, or "last updated"
  lines — git history records who and when.
- Add an item only for work the current change does not finish, and
  prefer an item here over a `# TODO` comment for anything a later
  session should pick up.
- Delete an item in the commit that resolves it — no "Done" section, no
  strikethrough — and add a `CHANGELOG.md` entry if users can see the
  result. An item dropped without being done is deleted the same way,
  with the reason in the commit message. Restore `_No entries yet._`
  when the list becomes empty.
{%- endif %}

### `README.md`

The root `README.md` is the landing page: what the project is, how to
{% if is_library %}install and use it{% else %}set it up and run it{% endif %}, and where to read further. It is not a
reference — signatures, options, and edge cases belong in docstrings.

Top to bottom, it holds:

1. The title — `name` in `pyproject.toml` — and the badges.
2. The one-line description in italics: word for word the
   `description` in `pyproject.toml`, trailing punctuation included,
   under 120 characters. Change the two together.
3. Optionally, a short paragraph with no heading — two to four
   sentences on scope, background, or who the project is for.
4. The lead sections, in this order:
{%- if is_library %}
   - 📦 Installation — the Python requirement, `uv add` / `pip install`,
     and each optional extra with what it adds.
   - 🚀 Quick start — one runnable example of the main use case.
     Examples for a single module belong in that module's README (see
     *Sub-READMEs*), not here.
   - 🧩 Modules — each public module or subpackage: its import path in
     backticks and what it offers, as a line, or as a short paragraph
     under a `###` heading once there is more to say.
{%- else %}
   - 📦 Setup — every step a fresh clone needs before the project runs:
     requirements (Python, hardware, drivers), `uv sync`, and one-time
     steps such as environment files or system libraries, each with its
     command.
   - 🚀 Usage — how to run the project: its commands, scripts, or entry
     points, and where their configuration lives.
   - 🗂️ Layout, once the tree no longer explains itself — one line per
     top-level directory a reader needs to find their way.
{%- endif %}

   The first entry in a section replaces its placeholder.
5. Project-specific sections, as the project needs them — a key
   concept, platform notes, acknowledgements — in the order a reader
   needs them, acknowledgements last.{% if is_library %} A command-line tool the
   library ships (`[project.scripts]`) comes first among them, as
   ⌨️ CLI: each command with one example.{% endif %}
6. The closing sections, always last and in this order:
   {% if include_todo %}📋 TODO, {% endif %}📜 Changelog, ⚖️ License.

- Every `##` heading starts with one emoji, as the template's do, and
  no two sections share one.
- Keep the template's sections, their names, and their order, and edit
  them in place; a project-specific section goes only where step 5
  puts it.
- No hand-written table of contents and no "back to top" links: GitHub
  gives every README an Outline button, and a root README long enough to
  need either is carrying material that belongs in a sub-README or in
  docstrings. Hand-made links to these headings also break silently: an
  emoji such as ⚖️ leaves an invisible U+FE0F in its anchor
  (`#️-license`){% if is_library %}, on GitHub and PyPI alike{% endif %}.
- Keep the Python requirement in the badge and in {% if is_library %}📦 Installation{% else %}📦 Setup{% endif %}
  in step with `requires-python` in `pyproject.toml`.
- A badge for a dependency — a framework, a CUDA version — goes right
  after the Python badge and shows the minimum in `pyproject.toml`; it
  moves with that minimum.
- Every code example runs against the current code: a change that breaks
  one updates it in the same commit. Show the result when it is the
  point, as a trailing `# -> ...` comment.
- Links stay valid: a commit that moves, renames, or deletes a file or
  directory fixes every README link to it — the root's hub links and
  the sub-READMEs' relative ones. `git grep -n '<old path>' -- '*.md'`
  finds them.
{%- if is_library %}
- The root README is also the PyPI project page, which has none of the
  repository's other files. Link a repository file by absolute URL —
  `https://github.com/{{ github_username }}/{{ repo_name }}/blob/main/<path>`, or
  `/tree/main/<dir>` for a directory — never by a relative path such as
  `./CHANGELOG.md`, which 404s there; in-page `#anchors` are fine. Leave
  out what PyPI cannot render: Mermaid diagrams, `<picture>`, and images
  by relative path.
{%- endif %}

#### Sub-READMEs

A {% if is_library %}subpackage{% else %}directory{% endif %} whose use outgrows its {% if is_library %}🧩 Modules{% else %}🗂️ Layout{% endif %} entry gets its
own `README.md` next to its code. The root then acts as a hub: the
entry keeps its summary and links to that README, and examples specific
to it move there.{% if not is_library %} A root README without 🗂️ Layout gains one for the
link.{% endif %}

A sub-README is shaped by what it documents — a file format, a family
of APIs, a pipeline step — so it has no fixed sections. It does keep to
these:

- The title names what it covers: {% if is_library %}the import path in backticks,
  as in `` # `{{ package_name }}.sub` ``{% else %}the directory, or a package's import
  path in backticks{% endif %}.
- One or two sentences under the title say what it covers and when to
  reach for it.
- It may go deeper than the root — concepts, worked examples, choosing
  between APIs, pitfalls — but leaves signatures and parameter details
  to docstrings and does not repeat the root.
- Its code examples run, under the same rule as the root's.
- Headings carry no emoji, so their anchors stay plain.
- Once it runs past a couple of screens, it opens with a `## Contents`
  list: a plain bullet list linking each `##` section, never folded into
  `<details>` — a contents list helps only while it is in view.
- Keep `## Contents` in step with the headings: a commit that adds,
  removes, renames, or reorders a `##` section updates the list. An
  anchor is the heading lowercased, spaces turned into hyphens, and
  punctuation other than `-` and `_` dropped:
  `` ## Resolving conflicts: `on_conflict` `` links as
  `#resolving-conflicts-on_conflict`.
- No "back to top" links: GitHub's file view has its own Top button.
{%- if is_library %}
- It may link relatively: only the root README reaches PyPI.
{%- endif %}

### `docs/decisions/`

Record a design decision — a choice between real alternatives that
later work should respect rather than reopen — as an Architecture
Decision Record: one file per decision at
`docs/decisions/NNNN-title-with-dashes.md`, numbered in sequence from
`0001` and never renumbered. Create the directory with the first record.
A rule for how code is written is not a decision record; it goes to
`AGENTS.md`.

Use the [MADR](https://adr.github.io/madr/) shape, kept short:

```markdown
# <Decision, stated as a short sentence>

- Status: accepted
- Date: YYYY-MM-DD

## Context and problem statement

## Considered options

## Decision outcome

## Consequences
```

- Write the record in the commit that implements the decision, or in
  its own `📝` commit when the decision comes first.
- A record is history: once accepted, change it only to fix a factual
  error. When a later decision reverses it, write a new record and set
  the old one's status to `superseded by NNNN`.

### `AGENTS.md`

- When a project-specific convention, command, or pitfall is settled —
  the user states a rule, or a non-obvious workflow is agreed on —
  record it below the `<!-- Add project-specific guidance below. -->`
  marker at the end of this file so later sessions follow it too.
- Leave everything above the marker as is: it is rendered from the
  copier template and refreshed by `copier update`, so local edits there
  turn into merge conflicts. If a template rule does not fit this
  project, add an overriding note below the marker and tell the user.

## Commit convention

Commit messages use a [Gitmoji](https://gitmoji.dev/) prefix and wrap
package/tool names in backticks:

```
<emoji> <Imperative summary; tool names in `backticks`>

<Optional body explaining *why*>
```

Common prefixes used in this project:

